<BaseLayout title="Mining Conveyors: Overland, Underground & Heavy-Duty Belt Systems | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Technical guide to mining conveyors: overland belt conveyors, underground transport, steel cord and multi-ply belting, troughing idlers, belt scales, dust suppression, and conveyor safety for ore, coal, and aggregate." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/industries/mining-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Mining Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Mining conveyors transport bulk materials, ore, coal, aggregate, overburden, and waste rock, across distances from hundreds of feet to several miles at throughputs of hundreds to thousands of tonnes per hour. These heavy-duty belt conveyor systems use reinforced belting (multi-ply fabric or steel cord), troughing idlers, high-powered drives, and robust steel structures to move abrasive, dense materials from the pit or underground face to processing plants, stockpiles, or load-out facilities. Systems range from short in-pit conveyors to multi-mile overland installations and are specified for surface mining, underground mining, and aggregate operations worldwide.</p>
<h2 id="overland-heading">Overland Conveyors</h2>
<p>Overland conveyors move material across the surface from the mine pit to processing plants, rail load-outs, or port facilities. Single-flight overland conveyors can span several miles, replacing haul truck fleets with continuous transport. The capital cost of an overland conveyor is significant, but on a long, fixed route its operating cost per tonne is typically lower than truck haulage because it avoids fuel, tires, drivers, and haul road maintenance.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/mining-overland-conveyor-hooded.webp" width="1000" height="561" alt="A long belt conveyor runs in a straight line across flat, bare ground toward distant hills, with curved metal hood covers over the belt and one open section showing material on the belt." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An overland conveyor crossing open ground at a mine site. Hood covers shield most of the belt, and the open section shows the loaded, troughed belt on its idlers. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TAKRAF_gearless_overland_conveyor_C1,_5.3_km,_11,000_tph_first_ore_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LComms, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Overland conveyor routing must follow terrain contours. Vertical curves (concave and convex) transition the belt over hills and through valleys. Horizontal curves allow the conveyor to change direction without transfer towers. Horizontally <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">curved conveyors</a> tilt the belt slightly at curves to keep material centered. Curve radii depend on belt width, belt tension, and material load: wider belts and higher tensions require larger radii. Steel cord belting for curved overland systems is engineered with specific transverse stiffness to maintain trough shape and material centering through horizontal curves.</p>
<p>Drive systems for overland conveyors use multiple drive stations distributed along the conveyor length. On long conveyors, a single head-end drive would need belt tension beyond practical belt ratings. Intermediate drives, called booster or tripper drives, apply additional pulling force along the route. Each drive station uses one or more large motors with fluid couplings or <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) for soft starting and speed control. Coordinated multi-drive control prevents belt tension spikes during starting and stopping.</p>
<h3>Belt Tension and Takeup</h3>
<p>Overland conveyors operate under high belt tension. Steel cord belts are rated by breaking strength in kN/m; Fenner Dunlop's <a href="https://www.ibtinc.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Fenner-Dunlop-Conveyor-Belt-Manual.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Conveyor Belt Manual</a> gives a typical range of ST500 to ST5400 kN/m and up. Working tension is set well below breaking strength by a service factor. Belt tension varies with load, elevation change, and ambient temperature. Gravity takeup systems use a weighted carriage on a vertical tower to maintain constant belt tension as the belt stretches and contracts. Hydraulic and winch-type takeup systems provide adjustable tension for conveyors with limited vertical space for gravity takeup towers.</p>
<p>Fenner Dunlop gives typical elongation for steel cord belting as less than 0.25%, and fabric belts stretch more. On a multi-mile conveyor, even a fraction of a percent adds up to many feet of belt. The takeup system must accommodate this stretch range plus additional travel for belt splicing and maintenance. Insufficient takeup travel causes belt sag between idlers, material spillage, and drive slippage.</p>
<h2 id="underground-heading">Underground Conveyors</h2>
<p>Underground mining conveyors transport ore and coal from the working face to the mine portal or shaft, where material transfers to surface conveyors or hoisting systems. Underground conveyors operate in confined spaces with low ceilings, tight curves, and limited access for installation and maintenance. Belt width is limited by entry dimensions and set by production requirements.</p>
<p>Fire safety dominates underground conveyor design. A belt fire in an underground mine produces toxic smoke that spreads rapidly through ventilation airways, creating a life-threatening hazard. In U.S. underground coal mines, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/75.1108" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 75.1108</a> requires every conveyor belt to be approved under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/part-14" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR Part 14</a>, MSHA's flame-resistance approval program, which sets the flame test in 30 CFR 14.20 and 14.22. <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/75.1731" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 75.1731</a> adds maintenance rules: damaged rollers that pose a fire hazard must be repaired or replaced immediately, belts must be aligned so they do not rub the structure, and splices must keep the belt flame-resistant. Belt covers use chloroprene or other flame-resistant compounds. Flame-resistant belts cost more than standard surface-mine belts. Other countries set their own fire-resistance tests. For more on <a href="/resources/conveyor-safety/">conveyor safety</a> practices, see our dedicated guide.</p>
<h3>Extensible Conveyors</h3>
<p>Continuous miners and longwall systems advance the working face deeper into the seam over time. The conveyor system must extend to follow the advancing face. Extensible (extendable) conveyors use a belt storage magazine at the tail end that stores extra belt in a series of loops. As the face advances, belt releases from the storage magazine and the tail structure moves forward. Storage capacity sets how far the conveyor can extend before a new belt section must be spliced in.</p>
<p>Underground conveyor structures use bolt-together steel frames designed for installation in confined tunnels. Components arrive in sections small enough to transport through mine entries. Assembly occurs underground using portable tools. Modular frame designs reduce underground installation time compared to welded structures by enabling bolt-together assembly with standard hand tools.</p>
<h3>Ventilation and Dust Control</h3>
<p>Underground conveyors share the mine ventilation system with personnel. Dust generated at transfer points, loading zones, and belt return areas enters the airstream and degrades air quality. Enclosed transfer chutes with dust curtains contain dust at loading and discharge points. Water sprays at transfer points suppress airborne dust before it enters the ventilation flow. Belt cleaning systems (primary and secondary scrapers) remove carryback material from the return belt to prevent dust generation along the return run.</p>
<h2 id="belting-heading">Heavy-Duty Belting</h2>
<p>Mining conveyor belts must withstand impact from large, heavy material; resist abrasion from sharp-edged ore and rock; maintain structural integrity under high tension over long distances; and survive environmental exposure to UV, moisture, and temperature extremes. Belt selection affects conveyor performance, maintenance cost, and operating life more than any other single component. For a broader look at belt materials and constructions, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">conveyor belting resource</a>.</p>
<h3>Multi-Ply Fabric Belts</h3>
<p>Multi-ply belts use layers of woven fabric (most commonly polyester warp and nylon fill, called EP fabric) bonded with rubber to form the belt carcass. Each ply adds strength. Fabric belts are rated by working tension in pounds per inch of width (PIW): Fenner Dunlop's example is 4 plies of 110 lb fabric making a 440 PIW belt. Multi-ply belts handle most in-plant and moderate-length mining conveyors. They splice with mechanical fasteners (for quick field replacement) or vulcanized splices (for longer life and higher strength).</p>
<p>The top cover, the rubber layer that contacts the material, resists abrasion and impact. Cover thickness ranges from thin covers for light-duty service to thick covers for heavy impact zones under crushers and chutes. Cover rubber grades vary by application: standard abrasion-resistant compounds handle most ore and aggregate; super-abrasion-resistant grades extend life in extremely abrasive applications such as sharp quartzite; heat-resistant covers handle material from kilns or sinter plants; and oil-resistant covers handle oil sands or oil-contaminated material.</p>
<h3>Steel Cord Belts</h3>
<p>Steel cord belts embed a single layer of parallel steel cables (cords) in rubber. The steel cords provide far higher strength and lower stretch than fabric plies. Steel cord construction is the standard for long-distance overland conveyors, high-lift conveyors, and any application where belt tension exceeds the capacity of multi-ply fabric belts.</p>
<p>Steel cord belts require vulcanized splices: the steel cords of two belt ends are overlapped and bonded under heat and pressure. A vulcanized steel cord splice takes many hours to complete in the field, requiring specialized equipment and trained technicians. Splice quality directly affects belt life; a poorly made splice is the weakest point and the most likely failure location. Belt suppliers typically provide factory-trained splice crews for critical installations to ensure splice integrity meets design specifications.</p>
<h3>Belt Rip Detection</h3>
<p>A longitudinal belt rip, caused by a sharp object penetrating the belt and slicing it lengthwise as the belt moves, can destroy a long length of expensive belting before anyone notices. Belt rip detection systems identify rips in progress and stop the conveyor before catastrophic damage occurs. Detection methods include embedded sensor loops in the belt (wire loops that break when the belt rips, triggering an alarm), surface-mounted rip sensors that detect belt edge damage, and vision systems that monitor the belt surface for tears. Rip detection systems integrate with conveyor control PLCs for automatic shutdown when a rip event is confirmed.</p>
<h2 id="idler-heading">Troughing Idlers &amp; Support Systems</h2>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/trough-conveyors/">Trough conveyors</a> shape the flat belt into a trough cross-section using angled idler rollers. The trough increases the belt's carrying capacity by containing material in a U-shaped channel instead of a flat surface. Standard trough angles are 20, 35, and 45 degrees (as offered in idler catalogs such as <a href="https://www.ppi-global.com/userdocs/literature/ppi/documents/idl_001-11_idler_catalog_web.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PPI's Belt Conveyor Idlers catalog</a>). Higher angles carry more material but require more flexible belting and generate higher edge stress.</p>
<p>A troughing idler set consists of three rollers: a flat center roller and two angled wing rollers. The center roller supports the belt bottom; the wing rollers lift the belt edges to form the trough. Idler sets mount on cross-frames along the carrying side. Return idlers (flat or V-shaped) support the empty belt on the return run underneath at wider spacing. Spacing is set from CEMA tables based on belt width, material weight, and belt tension.</p>
<div class="photo-row"><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/mining-schleenhain-troughed-belt.webp" width="1000" height="750" alt="A troughed conveyor belt loaded with dark material runs downhill beside a service road toward an open-pit mine, with closely spaced idler frames along both edges." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A loaded troughed belt at the Vereinigtes Schleenhain surface mine in Germany. The angled wing rollers along each edge hold the belt in its trough shape. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conveyor_belt_at_surface_mine_Schleenhain_13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Leonhard Lenz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/mining-ppi-troughing-idler.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Product render of a three-roll troughing idler: a horizontal center roll and two angled wing rolls mounted on a steel cross-frame with foot plates." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A three-roll troughing idler: a flat center roll and two angled wing rolls on one cross-frame. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.ppi-global.com/product-lines/idlers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PPI</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>Impact Idlers and Transition Zones</h3>
<p>Loading zones, where material falls onto the belt from chutes, feeders, or crushers, subject the belt and idlers to severe impact forces. Impact idlers use rubber-cushioned rollers or rubber disc assemblies that absorb shock and protect the belt from puncture and damage. Impact idlers are spaced much closer together than standard carrying idlers to distribute impact forces across more rollers.</p>
<p>Transition zones at the head and tail pulleys change the belt profile from troughed to flat (at the pulley) and back to troughed. The transition distance must be long enough to avoid overstressing the belt edges during the profile change. Insufficient transition distance causes belt edge buckling, premature edge wear, and splice failures. Engineering calculations set the minimum transition distance based on belt width, trough angle, and belt tension.</p>
<h3>Idler Maintenance and Failure</h3>
<p>A large mining conveyor has thousands of idler rollers. A single seized (frozen) idler creates a hot spot that can ignite the belt, a serious fire risk, especially underground, which is why <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/75.1731" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 75.1731</a> requires immediate repair of damaged rollers that pose a fire hazard in underground coal mines. Idler monitoring systems use thermal cameras, vibration sensors, or acoustic sensors to detect failing idlers before seizure. Infrared scanning systems mounted along the conveyor structure continuously monitor idler temperatures and alert maintenance when a roller exceeds temperature limits. Proactive idler replacement based on condition monitoring prevents belt fires and reduces unplanned downtime. For comprehensive component inspection strategies, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">conveyor maintenance guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="scale-heading">Belt Scales &amp; Monitoring</h2>
<p>Belt scales (also called belt weighers) measure the mass flow rate of material on a moving conveyor belt. The scale integrates belt load (weight per unit length) with belt speed to calculate tonnes per hour and cumulative tonnage. Mining operations use belt scale data for production tracking, process control (crusher feed rate, blend ratios), inventory management, and trade settlement (material sold by weight).</p>
<p>A belt scale consists of one or more weigh idler sets mounted on load cells, plus a belt speed sensor. The load cells measure the gravitational force of the material on the idlers. The speed sensor (typically a tachometer on the return belt or a speed wheel on the carry side) measures belt velocity. The scale integrator multiplies load by speed to produce a flow rate and totalizes cumulative weight over time.</p>
<h3>Scale Accuracy</h3>
<p>Belt scale accuracy depends on installation quality, calibration, and the number of weigh idlers. Multi-idler scales improve accuracy by averaging load measurements over a longer belt section. For example, Siemens rates its <a href="https://www.lesman.com/siemens-milltronics-msi-single-idler-and-mmi-multi-idler-heavy-duty-belt-scales" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Milltronics MSI</a> single-idler scale at ±0.5% or better and the MMI multi-idler scale at ±0.25% or better. Legal-for-trade scales typically use multi-idler configurations, regular calibration with test chains or known weights, and type approval such as NTEP (U.S.) or OIML.</p>
<p>Installation location affects accuracy. Belt scales should be installed on a straight section of conveyor with the length of straight, uniformly supported belt upstream and downstream that the scale manufacturer specifies. Curves, inclines, and belt tension variations near the scale introduce measurement errors. Conveyor structure rigidity matters, frame deflection under load changes the relationship between material weight and load cell readings.</p>
<h3>Belt Tracking and Alignment</h3>
<p>Belt mistracking, where the belt drifts to one side, spills material, damages belt edges, and causes structural damage to the conveyor frame. Mining conveyors use training idlers (self-aligning idlers) that pivot in response to belt drift and steer the belt back to center. Training idlers mount at regular intervals along the conveyor length. Severe mistracking triggers belt drift switches that stop the conveyor before the belt runs off the structure entirely. Belt alignment is critical for belt scale accuracy: a misaligned belt shifts load distribution across the weigh idlers and introduces measurement error.</p>
<h2 id="dust-heading">Dust Suppression &amp; Conveyor Galleries</h2>
<p>Dust is the primary environmental and health concern for mining conveyors. Material transfer points, where material drops from one conveyor to another through a chute, generate the most dust. Windblown dust from open belt runs adds to the problem. Mining operations must control dust to meet environmental permits, protect worker health, and maintain equipment reliability (dust infiltrates bearings, drives, and electrical systems).</p>
<h3>Transfer Point Dust Control</h3>
<p>Engineered transfer chutes control material trajectory and reduce dust generation at transfer points. A well-designed chute directs material smoothly from the discharge conveyor to the receiving conveyor, minimizing free-fall height and material turbulence. Modern transfer point engineering uses hood and spoon chute geometry, impact cradles, and stilling zones to reduce dust and material degradation at transfers.</p>
<p>Dust curtains (rubber flaps) at chute entry and exit points contain dust within the chute enclosure. Skirtboard sealing along the receiving belt edges prevents dust escape where the chute meets the belt. Primary and secondary belt scrapers on the discharge conveyor head pulley remove carryback material from the belt before it falls off the return run and creates dust. Belt cleaning systems use tungsten carbide or polyurethane scraper blades that conform to the belt surface for effective carryback removal.</p>
<h3>Water and Foam Suppression</h3>
<p>Water spray systems at transfer points and along belt runs wet the material surface to reduce airborne dust. Spray nozzles atomize water into fine droplets that bind with dust particles and prevent them from becoming airborne. The challenge is applying enough water to suppress dust without adding excessive moisture that affects downstream processing (screening, crushing, or material handling). Set the water rate with the downstream process engineer, since excess moisture can blind screens or cause buildup.</p>
<p>Foam suppression systems mix a surfactant with water to create a foam that spreads across the material surface. Foam covers more material surface per gallon of water than spray alone, so it can suppress dust while adding less moisture. That makes it useful where moisture addition must be minimized.</p>
<h3>Conveyor Galleries</h3>
<p>Conveyor galleries are enclosed structures (buildings) that house the conveyor belt run. Galleries contain windblown dust from open belt sections, protect the conveyor from weather (rain, snow, wind, UV exposure), and provide a protected walkway for maintenance access. Galleries are standard on overland conveyors near populated areas and in operations with strict environmental permit conditions.</p>
<p>Gallery construction ranges from simple corrugated metal enclosures to fully insulated, heated structures for arctic and cold-climate operations. Gallery design must allow access for belt replacement: one side wall or roof sections must be removable to thread new belt lengths through the structure. Ventilation within the gallery prevents dust accumulation and provides airflow for worker comfort during inspections. <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">Bucket elevators</a> at gallery elevation changes provide vertical material transport in compact footprints when conveyor incline angles exceed practical belt limits.</p>
<h2 id="safety-heading">Conveyor Safety Systems</h2>
<p>Mining conveyors present serious safety hazards: pinch points at pulleys and idlers, entanglement in moving belts, falling material from elevated structures, and fire risk from belt friction and seized idlers. Mining safety regulations (MSHA in the United States, and state or provincial mining regulations elsewhere) set specific requirements for conveyor guarding, emergency stops, and start-up warnings. For a complete overview of safety requirements, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-safety/">conveyor safety guide</a>.</p>
<h3>Pull-Cord Emergency Stop</h3>
<p>At U.S. surface metal and nonmetal mines, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/56.14109" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 56.14109</a> requires unguarded conveyors next to travelways to have either emergency stop devices located so a person falling on or against the conveyor can readily stop the drive, or railings that prevent people from falling against it. Pull-cord switches along the conveyor are the common way to meet the emergency stop option. Cords are tensioned so that a pull anywhere along the run trips a switch, and switch spacing follows the switch manufacturer's rating. Pull-cord switches typically latch in the tripped position, so the conveyor cannot restart until the switch is reset at the trip location.</p>
<h3>Guarding and Access</h3>
<p><a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/56.14107" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 56.14107</a> requires guards on drive, head, tail, and takeup pulleys and similar moving parts, unless the parts are at least seven feet from walking or working surfaces. Pinch points where the belt meets a pulley, where the belt contacts idler rollers, and at the takeup need guards that prevent body contact with moving parts. Guard design must allow inspection without removal (mesh guards provide visibility) and must be lockable to prevent unauthorized removal. Walkways alongside elevated conveyors require handrails, toe boards, and non-slip surfaces. Access platforms at drive stations, head pulleys, and tail pulleys provide safe maintenance positions.</p>
<h3>Monitoring and Interlocks</h3>
<p>Mining conveyor control systems interlock multiple safety devices: belt drift switches (stop the conveyor if the belt mistracks beyond a set limit), speed sensors (stop the conveyor if the belt stops moving while the drive motor runs, indicating a slipping belt), blocked chute detectors (stop the upstream conveyor if the discharge chute fills with material), and sequential start/stop systems that start conveyors downstream-to-upstream and stop upstream-to-downstream to prevent material pile-up.</p>
<p>Pre-start warning alarms (audible horns and visual beacons) alert personnel to stand clear. Under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/56.14201" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 56.14201</a>, when the whole conveyor is not visible from the starting switch, a visible or audible warning system is required, and the conveyor must start within 30 seconds of the warning or a second warning must be given. Most control systems interlock the warning so the belt cannot start without it.</p>
